  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $22.5B on contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department issued 30,008 awards with an average award size of $750K, and obligations were up 5.6% year over year. Contract obligations have declined from earlier peaks: FY21 obligations were $34.3B, FY22 obligations were $33.4B, FY23 obligations were $33.4B, FY24 obligations were $29.9B, and FY25 obligations were $21.3B. Information technology and professional services categories account for the largest shares of current-year obligations: Computer Systems Design Services obligations are $3.2B, Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services obligations are $2B, and Other Computer Related Services obligations are $1.9B. Research categories also appear prominently with Research and Development in Biotechnology obligations of $1.4B and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences obligations of $1.4B. The largest vendors by obligations in FY2026 to date include GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. with $956M and MANHATTAN TORCON A JOINT VENTURE with $492M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Department of Health and Human Services contract spending changed over recent fiscal years?",
        "body": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligations peaked in FY21 at $34.3B and trended lower through FY25 when obligations were $21.3B. FY26 to date obligations are $22.5B, which is up 5.6% year over year compared with the prior fiscal year. The Department issued 30,008 awards in FY2026 to date with an average award size of $750K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What categories and vendors account for the largest share of HHS obligations in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services is the largest category with $3.2B in obligations, followed by Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services with $2B and Other Computer Related Services with $1.9B. Research-related categories each show $1.4B in obligations for biotechnology and for physical, engineering, and life sciences research. GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. is the largest vendor with $956M in obligations, and MANHATTAN TORCON A JOINT VENTURE is second with $492M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Health and Human Services spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $22.5B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department issued 30,008 awards with an average award size of $750K, and obligations were up 5.6% year over year. Historical obligations were $34.3B in FY21, $33.4B in FY22, $33.4B in FY23, $29.9B in FY24, and $21.3B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Health and Human Services buy?",
      "a": "The Department of Health and Human Services buys a mix of IT, professional services, research, construction, and manufacturing-related goods and services. Top categories in FY2026 to date include Computer Systems Design Services at $3.2B, Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services at $2B, Other Computer Related Services at $1.9B, Custom Computer Programming Services at $1.5B, Research and Development in Biotechnology at $1.4B, Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $1.4B, and Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing at $692M."
